	issn = {0037-7732},
	journaltitle = {Social Forces},
	keywords = {group threat,perception,reviewed,use},
	note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (3/10/2022, 12:40:56 PM)} \par "Education, age and gender play key roles in the model. Education has a pronounced effect, with each increase of a year in school reducing the ratio of perceived minoritygroup size to that of whites by 5 percent. Gender has a sizable effect, also; men perceive relative minority-group size as 30 percent lower than do women. Age is not as strong a factor as these two, but older respondents do have more realistic perceptions. Even with these effects taken into account, blacks and Hispanics are more likely than whites to have distorted perceptions of the nation's demography. According to their coefficients, Hispanics report ratios of minority-to-white size that are 36 percent higher than whites do, while blacks report ratios that are 24 percent higher. However, two expectations do not hold up: the residents of rural areas, who presumably have less exposure to racial and ethnic diversity, are no more likely than others to have distorted perceptions of racial and ethnic group sizes; and the perceptions of the foreign born are not significantly different from those of the native born.' This measurement of this last effect may be biased by the exclusion of non-English speakers from the GSS universe." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/G87WIBWZ?page=8}{Alba et al 2005:908}) \par "he addition of the perceived neighborhood demographic context to the equation has relatively little impact on the coefficients of some predictors, but causes others to shift. Thu the effects of age, education and gender remain largely as they were in the earlier equatio Living in a rural area now has a significant effect, a positive one indicating that rural reside more likely to inflate minority-group sizes than are urbanites. But the largest changes occur the coefficients for the Hispanic-origin and black dummy variables. While they were large a positive in the earlier equation, now they are small and, in the case of blacks, insignificant. can conclude that the more severe misperceptions of group sizes by black and Hispanic respondents were largely due to their residential contexts. Because of residential segregat their communities have far fewer white residents and far more minority-group ones than do neighborhoods in which whites reside (Massey and Denton 1993; Logan 2001). With neighborhood context controlled, whites and members of minority groups are about equally likely to hold distorted perceptions of the racial/ethnic composition of the nati" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/G87WIBWZ?page=10}{Alba et al 2005:910}) \par "This finding does not imply, however, that the attitudinal effects of group-size perceptions are attributable to community contexts. Our analyses show that, ev with the compositions of these contexts controlled, misperceptions have significant influences on attitudes for members of the majority group" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/G87WIBWZ?page=14}{Alba et al 2005:914}) \par \textbf{Extracted Annotations (3/14/2022, 5:01:45 PM)} \par "Education, age and gender play key roles in the model. Education has a pronounced effect, with each increase of a year in school reducing the ratio of perceived minoritygroup size to that of whites by 5 percent. Gender has a sizable effect, also; men perceive relative minority-group size as 30 percent lower than do women. Age is not as strong a factor as these two, but older respondents do have more realistic perceptions. Even with these effects taken into account, blacks and Hispanics are more likely than whites to have distorted perceptions of the nation's demography. According to their coefficients, Hispanics report ratios of minority-to-white size that are 36 percent higher than whites do, while blacks report ratios that are 24 percent higher. However, two expectations do not hold up: the residents of rural areas, who presumably have less exposure to racial and ethnic diversity, are no more likely than others to h