	langid = {english},
	note = {\section{Annotations\\ (4/26/2022, 11:34:06 PM)} \par ``but researchers have failed to conduct full critical analyses of how social isolation operates. Two major issues have been ignored. First, research focuses on residential neighborhoods as the sole relevant context of isolation, thereby ignoring the character and influence of other locations where people go. Individuals may spend time in a variety of neighborhoods when they travel to work, shop, worship and conduct other regular activities (Kwan 2012).'' (Krivo et al., 2013, p. 142) \par ``Second, social isolation is treated as a condition experienced only by disadvantaged individuals and communities. This overlooks how isolation is reproduced among both the disadvantaged and well off. Because the affluent may also be isolated in highly advantaged settings, a system'' (Krivo et al., 2013, p. 142) \par ``Relying on census tracts as the neighborhoods where activities take place raises potential concerns about the modifiable area unit problem. An activity such as grocery shopping may be identified as occurring outside of one's home neighborhood when one has simply crossed a tract boundary that is not truly socially meaningful. Some studies utilize Geographic Information Systems technology to provide refined neighborhood definitions of varying scales for analyses of residential segregation (e.g., Reardon and Bischoff 2011; Reardon et al. 2008). Although we do not apply this approach, the relatively long distances travelled to conduct activities suggest that the use of census tracts is not the major reason for our findings. On average, adults in this sample travel almost 5 miles to the activities. The mean distance varies by activity from 1.7 miles to the grocery store to 9.3 to work. These average distances are notably larger than the size of the vast majority of the 65 home tracts in the sample; three quarters are less than 1 square mile.8'' (Krivo et al., 2013, p. 150) \par ``Car access is associated with lower disadvantage in neighborhoods visited.'' (Krivo et al., 2013, p. 156) should have included distance to bus stop and route density for Transit since they had these people's addresses \par ``e for Observed Levels of Home Tract Disadvantage'' (Krivo et al., 2013, p. 157) why are the Black and Latino lines the same \par ``The findings are instructive. There is a close connection between disadvantage in individuals' home neighborhoods and their nonhome activity locations. This association holds for residents of neighborhoods at all disadvantage levels and for all race-ethnic groups. However, the use of urban space is also racialized. African Americans and Latinos conduct activities in areas that are significantly more disadvantaged than areas where whites spend time even when living in economically similar neighborhoods. Individuals living in more heavily African American and Latino neighborhoods suffer an additional increase in the level of disadvantage that they experience in their movement through the city.'' (Krivo et al., 2013, p. 159) \par ``Second, it is important to extend accessibility to all areas of the city for all economic and ethnoracial groups. This may involve providing a broad network of affordable and convenient public transportation to facilitate people getting wherever they want/need to go.'' (Krivo et al., 2013, p. 160)},
