	journaltitle = {The Public Opinion Quarterly},
	keywords = {group threat,perception,use},
	note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (3/14/2022, 5:02:52 PM)} \par "possibility, suggested by Fossett and Kiecolt (1989, p. 825), is that the metropolitan area is the most appropriate contextual unit for metropolitan residents because it most accurately reflects the "life space" that urban residents inhabit. Practi" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/RXU755SM?page=7}{Nadeau et al 1993:338}) \par "classic work of Allport and his associates (e.g., Pettigrew, Allport, and Barnett 1958), for example, suggests that people who feel threatened by minorities are the most likely to overestimate their numbers. Citing" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/RXU755SM?page=11}{Nadeau et al 1993:342}) \par "To investigate the possible links between perceptions of threat and minority group size, we examined two items referring to blacks and four referring to Hispanics. For blacks, the first item-whether civil rights leaders are pushing too fast-was the most direct and was part of Giles and Evans's (1985, p. 55) measurement of threat. No other available items tapped threat directly, but one item-about preferential hiring and promotion-reflects a major way in which black advancement threatens some whites.10" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/RXU755SM?page=11}{Nadeau et al 1993:342}) \par "Hispanics, we used items asking" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/RXU755SM?page=11}{Nadeau et al 1993:342}) \par "whether their growing numbers threaten current residents' jobs, lead to increased taxes and crime, and threaten the status of English as our common language.11" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/RXU755SM?page=12}{Nadeau et al 1993:343}) \par "It would appear, then, that people who feel threatened by minorities perceive greater numbers of them (especially when they are in areas of objectively high minority concentrations). As noted, the connection may be one of cause or effect. What we can establish for now is that the connection indeed exists, even after controlling for other possible explanatory factors." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/RXU755SM?page=13}{Nadeau et al 1993:344})},
	number = {3},
