	abstract = {In The Problem With Feeding Cities, Andrew Deener describes the historical development of the infrastructure that enables food distribution in the United States. Although Deener takes Philadelphia as his starting point, the book is less about the city itself than it is the evolving infrastructural network that feeds it. But food distribution is a challenging subject---in part because, as Deener notes, ``unlike water or electricity, food is not one `thing''' (3). This is particularly the case for perishable ``fresh'' foods, which decompose at variable rates depending on the product type and environmental conditions.},
