@article{vallasReviewWhatWorkers2023,
	abstract = {There is a long tradition of writing that seeks to foreground the voices of subaltern agents, enriching the public's understanding of how workers and other marginalized groups experience and respond to the world. Oral histories are premised on this effort; John Gwaltney's Drylongso showcased the meanings that many African Americans share among themselves; Studs Terkel's famous Working was so successful at capturing the everyday concerns of workers than it was made into a Broadway musical. Enter Roberta Iversen's What Workers Say. The book's premise is that policymakers would do well to understand the life horizons of workers in a broad array of occupations and industries, especially those in the burgeoning service sector, where those without a college education have enduring stagnating wages, eroding provisions for upward mobility, and an uphill battle to stay above the poverty line.},
